<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-simple-algebras" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Simple algebras</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
        We introduce <sq>simple algebras</sq> in this section. Although the term <sq>semisimple algebra</sq>, introduced earlier, suggests that it is a generalization of simple algebra, it is not true without some additional conditions.
    </p>
    <p>
        We assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity and <m>A</m> is an <m>R</m>-algebra.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <definition xml:id="def-simple-algebra">
    <title>Simple algebra</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            An <m>R</m>-algebra <m>A</m> is said to be <em>simple</em> if its only two-sided ideals are <m>\{0\}</m> and <m>A</m> itself.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
    <title>Quaternion algebra is simple over a field of characteristic not <m>2</m></title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            We have shown in <xref ref="prop-quaternion-algebra-is-simple"/> that the quaternion algebra <m>(a,b)_F</m> over a field of characteristic not equal to <m>2</m> is a simple algebra.
        </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  <p>
    In the next propostion we characterize simple algebras that are semisimple.
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-simple-iff-semisimple-artinian">
    <statement>
        <p>
            For a simple algebra <m>A</m>, following are equivalent:
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> is semisimple;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A_A</m> is right Artinian;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>A</m> has a minimal right ideal.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <proof>
    <p>
        Suppose that <m>A</m> is a simple algebra as well as a semisimple algebra. We show that <m>A_A</m> is right Artinian. Note that for any nonzero <m>a\in A</m>, the two-sided ideal <m>AaA</m> is nonzero and hence, <m>AaA=A</m>. Therefore, there are <m>x_i,y_i\in A</m> such that <m>1=\sum_{i=1}^n x_iay_i</m>. This implies that <m>A_A</m> is generated by <m>\{x_1a,x_2a,\ldots,x_na\}</m>, i.e., <m>A_A</m> is finitely generated. In view of <xref ref="thm-semisimple-artinian-noetherian-modules"/>, <m>A_A</m> is right Artinian. This shows that (1) implies (2).
    </p>
    <p>
        If <m>A_A</m> is right Artinian then, by <xref ref="lem-existence-of-minimal-maximal-element"/>, <m>A_A</m> has a minimal right ideal. This shows that (2) implies (3).
    </p>
    <p>
        Now assume that <m>N</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>. Hence, <m>N\neq 0</m> and <m>AN</m>, a two-sided ideal of <m>A</m> generated by <m>N</m>, is <m>A</m>. Thus, <me>
            A=\sum_{a\in A}aN.
        </me>
        Note that each <m>aN</m> is a right ideal of <m>A</m>, i.e., each <m>aN</m> is an <m>A</m>-module. Furthermore, each <m>aN\simeq N</m> as an <m>A</m>-modules. Hence, <m>A</m> is written as a sum of simple <m>A</m>-modules, i.e., <m>A</m> is a semisimple algebra. This proves that (3) implies (1).
    </p>
  </proof>
</section>